Without trying to reach the same times as C on POSIX:

$ time find ~/src/public/lisp/ -type f -name \*.asd > /tmp/asd-files

real	0m0.016s
user	0m0.002s
sys	0m0.013s


bocl> (time (defparameter *asd-files* (directory #P"~/src/public/lisp/**/*.asd")))

;; Elapsed: 86.970 s

*ASD-FILES*


reducing the time by two order of magnitude when the difference is
four should be feasible easily enough, and is a reasonable expectation.



----------------------------------------------------------------------------
FIXED in v1.4.38.

The Lisp DIRECTORY walked the tree with %GLOB, which per directory entry paid
interpreter-funcall overhead (~150 us each) many times over: a CHAR-by-CHAR
%WILD-MATCH, a %DIRECTORY-P stat() syscall, string CONCATENATE, an O(n^2) APPEND,
and %LIST-DIRECTORY twice per level.  Two changes:

1. New kernel primitive %DIRECTORY-GLOB (sources/bocl.c) runs the whole recursive
   glob walk in C: opendir/readdir once per level, readdir's d_type (stat only
   for symlinks / DT_UNKNOWN), a byte-wise glob matcher, matches collected into a
   C string vector during the walk (no Lisp allocation -> no GC / longjmp
   mid-walk), and the Lisp result list built once at the end.  DIRECTORY now calls
   it instead of the Lisp %GLOB.

2. DIRECTORY built each result with the CL-level PATHNAME, which first runs
   logical-pathname host/device probes (%LP-PARSE-HOST/DEVICE) that can never
   match a filesystem path — ~80x of the per-result cost — before calling the
   kernel pathname constructor anyway.  DIRECTORY results are always physical
   absolute paths, so it now maps *%KERNEL-PATHNAME-FN* directly (identical
   result, no logical probing).

Measured (A/B, same synthetic tree, 123 matches):
  OLD (%glob + #'pathname)   55.9 s
  NEW (directory)             0.011 s      (~5000x)
Of the new 0.011 s the C walk is ~0.015 s on a larger 4417-file tree and the
pathname construction ~0.006 s; both now scale like `find`.
